FONTS
=====

The logo and the icon are set in Panchang, a typeface by Indian Type Foundry
(ITF), distributed by Fontshare <https://www.fontshare.com/fonts/panchang>
under the ITF Free Font License; ITF owns its trademarks and copyright. The
logo and icon files contain outlines and pixels only: the font itself is not
distributed with this program (it is not in the executable and must not be
committed to the repository; scripts/make_brand.py looks for it locally).

The font/ folder and the executable contain the DejaVu Sans fonts
<https://dejavu-fonts.github.io/>, used for the texts written on the videos.
They are (c) Bitstream (Bitstream Vera) and Tavmjong Bah (Arev glyphs); the
DejaVu changes are in the public domain. Their license is in
font/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt and travels with them.

The icons of the toolbar come from Segoe Fluent Icons / Segoe MDL2 Assets,
fonts installed with Windows: they are used from the system and are not
distributed with this program.


THIRD-PARTY SOFTWARE
====================

EditSimple does not include FFmpeg. When the user asks for it, the program
downloads the official Windows build ("release essentials", by Gyan Doshi,
<https://www.gyan.dev/ffmpeg/builds/>, the build linked from
<https://ffmpeg.org/download.html>) into the tools/ffmpeg/ folder next to
it, keeping ffmpeg.exe together with its LICENSE and README files. FFmpeg
<https://ffmpeg.org> is a separate program under the GNU LGPL v2.1 or later
(GPL v3 when built with GPL components, as that build is); it is not part of
this program and is run as an external executable for the export. FFmpeg's
own license and source code are available at <https://ffmpeg.org/legal.html>.
An ffmpeg already present in the PATH is used in the same way.

The Python package opencv-python-headless ships its own copy of the FFmpeg
libraries (opencv_videoio_ffmpeg*.dll, GNU LGPL v2.1 or later), which
EditSimple uses through OpenCV to read video frames for the preview, the
timeline and the subject tracking. The executable built with PyInstaller
contains that library unmodified, as a separate DLL; its license is in the
LICENSE-3RD-PARTY.txt file of opencv-python-headless, copied in the
licenses folder next to the executable. Since EditSimple is free software,
anyone can rebuild the executable with a different or modified copy of it.

EditSimple uses the Python libraries CustomTkinter (CC0 1.0 in version
6.0.0, MIT in earlier versions, with its dependencies darkdetect,
BSD-3-Clause, and packaging, Apache 2.0 or BSD-2-Clause), Pillow (MIT-CMU),
NumPy (BSD-3-Clause, with bundled components under 0BSD, MIT, Zlib and
CC0 1.0) and OpenCV (opencv-python-headless, Apache License 2.0; the codecs
and libraries built into it are under their own licenses, listed in its
LICENSE-3RD-PARTY.txt). Each of them remains under its own license, as does
the Python interpreter (PSF License). The executable built with PyInstaller
contains them unmodified; scripts/build_exe.py copies their license files
into the licenses folder next to the executable.

Audio playback in the preview uses the Windows multimedia API (winmm), part
of the operating system.


TRACKING MODEL (DOWNLOADED ON REQUEST)
======================================

The subject tracking can use VitTrack (vision transformer tracker), a neural
network model that is not part of this program and is not included in the
executable. It is optional: without it EditSimple tracks with template
matching only. When the user asks for it, the program downloads
object_tracking_vittrack_2023sep.onnx from the official OpenCV model zoo
<https://github.com/opencv/opencv_zoo/tree/main/models/object_tracking_vittrack>
into the tools/vittrack/ folder next to it, checks it against the SHA-256
fingerprint written in editsimple/tools.py, and saves next to it its license
(Apache License 2.0) and a README.txt with its origin. The model was
contributed by Pengyu Liu in Google Summer of Code 2023 for the OpenCV
project; it is run by the DNN module of OpenCV as published.


